<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Classical antiquity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE–<a href="/years/476">476</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as classical era, classical period, classical age, antiquity</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Classical antiquity is the period of European cultural history between the 8th century BCE and the 5th century CE, comprising the interwoven civilizations of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world">Greco-Roman world</a>. It is conventionally taken to begin with the earliest recorded epic poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and to end with the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> in 476. Greek culture, with some influences from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>, was the basis of art, philosophy, society and education in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> until the Roman imperial period, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> spread it throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Surviving fragments of classical culture helped produce the revival beginning in the 14th century later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p></div>
